Lua 5.4.9 for P32W Unix
======================

Source: the official release, https://www.lua.org/ftp/lua-5.4.9.tar.gz,
SHA-256 2335b6c582a52654f94612bf10d2f4672805d05329aa6568b1d8cd9e5c6fb8e6
(checked against lua.org's published value). Here are its src/*.c and
src/*.h (not lua.hpp, the Makefile or doc/).

Lua is free software: Copyright (C) 1994-2026 Lua.org, PUC-Rio, under the
MIT licence, whose text is at the end of lua.h (kept as it is). The P32W
files (lp32.c, lp32.h, test/, this README) are under the same
licence.

Configuration
-------------
- lua_Integer is int (32 bits), lua_Number double (P32W's double is
  IEEE binary64 in software, softfp, D27/D30): luaconf.h picks
  LUA_INT_INT when __P32__ (or LUA_P32HOST) is defined.
- The end of luaconf.h ("Local configuration") includes lp32.h, which
  sets the rest: no locale (the decimal point is '.'), LUAI_MAXSTACK
  16000, package.path /usr/lib/lua/5.4/?.lua ... ./?.lua, no C modules
  (no LUA_USE_DLOPEN, no LUA_USE_POSIX), io.popen by libc's popen,
  os.tmpname by mkstemp, isatty for the interactive check. Everything
  else (system, tmpfile, setvbuf, freopen, strcoll, clock, difftime,
  strftime, <locale.h>, sig_atomic_t) is the C library's own.
- Numbers use the C library's floating point (libc/fpconv.c, fpfmt.c,
  fpmath.c, math.c): exact strtod, printf %e %f %g %a exactly as the
  Mac prints them, math.h.

Changes to Lua's own files (each marked /* P32W: ... */)
------------------------------------------------------
- luaconf.h: LUA_INT_DEFAULT is LUA_INT_INT on P32W; lp32.h included at
  the end.
- lstrlib.c: string.pack/unpack "f" is 4-byte IEEE single precision
  (lp32_tof32/lp32_fromf32), since P32W's float is the 8-byte double.
- lauxlib.c is Lua's own. (Lua used to have its own allocator here,
  lp32_alloc, because libc's malloc walked its free list on every free:
  a full GC after a deep recursion took 113 s of P32W time. libc's
  malloc now frees in constant time (D32), and Lua is faster without it.)

Build with -I lua. (Lua's first port carried the C library functions
P32W lacked, and a <locale.h>, in lp32.c and p32inc/; they are in libc
now.)

Building (host, as software/Makefile does)
------------------------------------------
  for each lua/*.c except lua.c, luac.c:
      python3 ../cc/p32cc.py -w -I lua -c lua/X.c -o build/lua/X.o
  python3 ../assembler/p32ar.py build/liblua.a build/lua/*.o (the core)
  lua:  p32cc lua.o build/liblua.a      luac: p32cc luac.o build/liblua.a

The native cc on P32W compiles every file, with the same output as the
host's (lvm.c, the biggest, in 3 MB: D33). (It once ran out of memory
on five of them, and its #if arithmetic was 32-bit, which got
lopcodes.h's limits wrong; both are fixed, D30 and D33.)

A test build on another machine (same configuration, the host's libc):
  cc -DLUA_P32HOST -DLUA_USE_POSIX -I lua -o lua32 lua/*.c (without luac.c) -lm

Tests
-----
test/*.lua and test/cmdline.sh; test/mksh.sh puts them together as
software/systest/cmd/lua.sh, whose .expect was recorded on P32W (run
twice, identical). The output matches the host test build's except for
strerror's wording, os.setlocale and package.path. test/bench.lua gives
rough speeds.
